#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078199 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078544 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06ab388a-b152-58aa-9764-ba4f8cabdf11" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "4d69af5a-1ec2-53b7-ad94-b292c6beaf47" :question/math-academy-id "e-1502" :question/problem "Find the common ratio of the following geometric sequence.\n$90, \\: -30, \\: 10, \\: -\\dfrac{10}{3}, \\: \\ldots$" :question/worked-solution "The common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence is the constant we multiply by to get to the next term. In other words, it is the ratio of consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common ratio for our sequence is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{(-30)}{90} = -\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078200 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078545 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c5580d5-b666-5e95-b841-1813c1a71b84" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Term"}] :question/id #uuid "5d76d255-8f67-5f95-b789-b4840469dfc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-1504" :question/problem "Find the $7$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n$1, \\: 2, \\: 4, \\: 8, \\: \\ldots$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/65/653f9ccfe28f5e3630afd30562064f1d790245b227896ea7db2da4265f7bac1a.png)\n\nNotice that the sequence is geometric, with a common ratio of\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{2}{1}=2\n$$\n\nSo, we can get each term by multiplying the previous term by $2$.\n\nContinuing to list terms of the sequence until we get to the $7$th term, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/6a/6ae31d38f90d943b5bd802652d0dedd4925351b1ffadd6398d018a1dd4c7f0d3.png)\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is $64$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078201 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434acf34-51e5-5733-a50d-a5199def8c77" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Geometric Sequences"}] :question/id #uuid "f25cd97e-0a26-5b3b-a4f7-aef8acb561a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-1505" :question/problem "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\n1. $2, \\: 4, \\: 8, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$\n2. $1, \\: -\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\: \\dfrac{1}{9}, \\: -\\dfrac{1}{27}, \\: \\ldots$\n3. $4, \\: 8, \\: 12, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n$\\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = \\frac{4}{2}|= 2; \\frac{a_{3}}{a_{2}} = \\frac{8}{4}|= 2; \\frac{a_{4}}{a_{3}} = \\frac{16}{8}|= 2$\n- Sequence II is also geometric. Again, for each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n$\\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = ((-\\frac{1}{3}))/(1)|=-\\frac{1}{3}; \\frac{a_{3}}{a_{2}} = ((\\frac{1}{9}))/((-\\frac{1}{3}))|=-\\frac{1}{3}; \\frac{a_{4}}{a_{3}} = ((-\\frac{1}{27}))/((\\frac{1}{9}))|=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n- Sequence III is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n$\\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = \\frac{8}{4}|= 2; \\frac{a_{3}}{a_{2}} = \\frac{12}{8}|= \\frac{3}{2}$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequences I and II could be geometric sequences."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078214 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078546 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20303666-34cb-53cc-9d00-2f472e0bb315" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable Using the Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "ad13200a-0939-5b3e-a678-7db922cf0b5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1796" :question/problem "Given that $x$, $3x$, $36$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x$." :question/worked-solution "The first three terms are as follows:\n\n$$\na_{1} = x, a_{2} = 3x, a_{3} = 36\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\nr = \\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = \\frac{3x}{x} = 3\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\nr = \\frac{a_{3}}{a_{2}} = \\frac{36}{3x} = \\frac{12}{x}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 &= \\frac{12}{x} \\\\\n3x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078545 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c5580d5-b666-5e95-b841-1813c1a71b84" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ceff70a8-de66-5f92-b01d-f66ba9169bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077241} {:answer/id #uuid "f1332d1f-d414-5f55-be3e-9c7ab440b5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077242} {:answer/id #uuid "f4feeef8-3156-5c08-8feb-eb7d0a1dd4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077243} {:answer/id #uuid "4920fd01-08c5-54d9-b99e-829f9f4be665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077244} {:answer/id #uuid "650c0a51-6831-56e7-8bb6-5c93983840ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077245}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0a14d464-fffd-5524-9ec5-b14f163649ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077240}] :question/id #uuid "40cd52fc-c3d5-5074-bd5e-a3c26e4deaea" :question/math-academy-id "q-5241" :question/problem "Find the $5$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n$2,-4, 8,-16, …$"}]}
